Star catalog provenance
=======================

bright-<hash>.bin
  Magnitude-sorted naked-eye catalog, 12,335 stars in the STR2 format: a
  J2000/ICRS direction, apparent magnitude, display color, and the catalog id
  the star came from (Gaia DR3 source_id, or Hipparcos number). The filename
  carries the sha256 of its own bytes, as every fetched sky asset does.

  Built offline by tools/starcat/build_bright.py from the snapshots committed
  under tools/starcat/data/ — ESA Gaia DR3 gaia_source (source_id, ra, dec,
  phot_g_mean_mag, bp_rp, parallax; phot_g_mean_mag < 6.5) and the Hipparcos
  New Reduction (hip, ra, dec, hp_mag, b_v, plx, pm_ra, pm_de; hp_mag < 6.5).
  The web application never contacts ESA or CDS at runtime.

  The two are merged in three tiers. Gaia measures the catalog. Brighter than
  Hp 2.5 it cannot — DR3 saturates above G 1.7 and its photometry is unreliable
  past that — so the Hipparcos row is authoritative and the Gaia row is dropped.
  Fainter than that the Hipparcos row is only *added*, where no Gaia row lies
  within 3 arcseconds and 1.5 magnitudes of it. 198 stars are there on those
  terms, including five of the second magnitude — Sheratan, Menkar, Mahasim,
  Gienah and Enif — which the earlier Hp < 2.5 supplement cut just above and
  Gaia records only as saturated pixels. Where such a star is added, any Gaia
  record within 3 arcseconds of it is dropped whatever its magnitude says: that
  record is the same star measured badly, and the disagreement is why the star
  was added at all.

  Hipparcos positions are epoch 1991.25 and Gaia DR3's are 2016.0, so every
  Hipparcos row is carried forward 24.75 years by its own proper motion before
  it is matched or stored. Epsilon Cygni moves 480 mas/yr: uncorrected it lands
  twelve arcseconds from its Gaia record, four times the match radius, and
  would be added a second time and drawn twice.

  Display color is derived, not drawn: BP-RP (or B-V for the Hipparcos rows)
  to an effective temperature, then a blackbody spectrum through the CIE 1931
  observer to sRGB, quantized onto 24 levels. The relations are cited in
  tools/starcat/colour.py — Mucciarelli & Bellazzini 2020 (RNAAS 4, 52) for
  BP-RP, Ballesteros 2012 (EPL 97, 34008) for B-V, and Wyman, Sloan & Shirley
  2013 (JCGT 2(2)) for the color matching functions.

Named-star callouts
  named-<hash>.json holds 339 stars, position-matched to bright.bin and
  built by tools/starcat/build_names.py: every name in the IAU Catalog of Star
  Names whose star is in the catalogue, plus Regor and R Doradus, which the IAU
  list does not carry. The ~110 that do not resolve are fainter than the
  catalogue's magnitude 6.5 limit. Constellation comes from the IAU row's own
  three-letter code; classification is HYG v3's spectral type read as a phrase;
  distance is HYG's parallax distance in light years, rounded for legibility
  (a tenth under 100 ly, ten under 1,000, a hundred beyond).

  The fifty entries the site opened with keep their own text. They were checked
  one at a time against SIMBAD and the Hipparcos New Reduction (CDS VizieR
  I/311); where the generated phrase differs it differs in wording, and a build
  should not overwrite a person's reading. They live at
  tools/starcat/data/named_hand.json, which is the build's input and not its
  output. Distances for luminous supergiants whose parallax is poorly
  constrained are approximate rather than false-precision reciprocals.

Constellation figures
  sky/lines-<hash>.bin is 674 segments over the 88 constellations, packed by
  tools/starcat/build_lines.py as pairs of bright.bin record indices. The
  figures are Stellarium's modern sky culture (v23.4
  skycultures/modern/constellationship.fab), which that file's info.ini
  licenses CC BY-SA 4.0 International Public License + Free Art License; the
  Hipparcos numbers it draws to are resolved into catalogue records through
  HYG v3 positions. Two of Stellarium's 676 segments are absent, both of them
  in Canis Major and both through HIP 33165, a V 6.65 star past the catalogue's
  own magnitude 6.5 limit; a segment that does not resolve at both ends is
  refused rather than guessed. The other nine that used to be missing are now
  drawn — their stars are what the catalogue fill added. public/sky/lines.json
  records the counts, the unresolved HIP numbers, and the content hash of the
  catalogue whose record indices the file addresses.

